  "leadSense": {
    "_why": "Which of a word's OWN senses should lead, for the few where Wiktionary's order misleads. This invents nothing: the value must match a sense the dictionary already lists, and the only effect is to move it to the front. Needed because Wiktionary glosses an abbreviation's expansion as an ordinary word — `no` carries both 'abbreviation of noroeste' AND 'northwest', so demoting metalinguistic TEXT still left the learner told that `no` means northwest. Confined to high-frequency function words, where the wrong lead is met constantly and is also the answer shown in flashcards, popups and level tests. Only reordering is possible here: `a` is left alone because the extract has no preposition sense for it at all, and inventing one would break the rule that a definition comes from the dictionary or not at all.",
    "es": {
      "e": "and",
      "no": "not",
      "o": "or",
      "se": "A reflexive or reciprocal pronoun",
      "so": "whoa",
      "u": "or",
      "y": "and"
    },
    "fr": {
      "ne": "not, no",
      "se": "The third-person reflexive",
      "y": "there (at a place)",
      "à": "to (destination)",
      "ce": "this, that",
      "yaourt": "yoghurt",
      "sol": "floor",
      "salle": "room (in a house)",
      "marron": "chestnut brown",
      "rouge": "red",
      "bureau": "office (room)"
    }
  },
  "curatedGloss": {
    "_why": "HAND-WRITTEN definitions, for the rare entry whose source is missing a core sense outright or pads it with something a learner should never be shown. Everything else in this app follows a strict rule — a definition comes from the licensed dictionary or it does not exist — and this section is the one deliberate exception, so keep the list tiny and justify every line. Prefer `leadSense` whenever the sense merely needs promoting: that only reorders what the dictionary already says and stays inside the rule. Each value REPLACES the entry's whole gloss, so senses omitted here are deleted.",
    "es": {
      "a": "to, at",
      "primera": "first",
      "segunda": "second",
      "tercera": "third",
      "tía": "aunt",
      "prima": "female cousin; bonus, premium",
      "chica": "girl",
      "novia": "girlfriend; bride",
      "maestra": "teacher (female); schoolmistress"
    },
    "fr": {
      "une": "a, an (feminine); front page (of a publication)",
      "la": "the (feminine singular); her, it (direct object)",
      "pas": "not (paired with ne); step, pace, footstep",
      "nous": "we, us",
      "vous": "you (plural, or polite singular)",
      "assiette": "plate; seat",
      "chambre": "bedroom; room, chamber",
      "rose": "pink; rose (flower)",
      "ci": "this one, here (as in celui-ci, ce livre-ci)",
      "donne": "gives, give; deal (in cards)"
    }
  },
  "inheritLemmaSense": {
    "_why": "Feminine/plural ADJECTIVE forms that are headwords in their own right but lost their everyday meaning. Wiktionary files 'feminine singular of blanco' as a form_of sense, which the build routes into the FORMS map rather than the gloss (correctly — it is morphology, not a definition), so the entry keeps only whatever niche noun senses it also had: `fria` was defined as \"beer\", `rica` as \"fenugreek\", `mexicana` as \"A robbery of drugs.\", and `cubana` as a vulgar sex act and nothing else. These are NOT hand-written definitions — the lemma's own dictionary gloss is prepended, so the strict-dictionary rule holds: `fria` gets exactly what `frio` says. That is only valid for adjectives, where the feminine means the same thing; a gendered NOUN pair does not work this way (tia is not 'uncle'), so those live in curatedGloss instead. scripts/audit-glosses.mjs finds candidates — 226 of them in Spanish — but the list is REVIEWED, never applied wholesale: most are correct nominalizations (`cara` = face, `musica` = music, `entrada` = entrance) that prepending would actively break.",
